# Video encoder profile — sourced by gw-keepalive.sh on EVERY gateway start, so switching is
# "edit this file, kill the gateway, the keepalive brings it back with the new numbers".
#
# The knob that matters most is VIDEO_TARGET_KBS, and not for the reason its name suggests.
# The sender paces a large frame's packets at that rate, and it sleeps to do so IN THE ENCODE
# LOOP — so the target rate also decides how often the desktop is looked at at all.  At 150 KB/s
# an 80 KB frame costs the loop ~550 ms of sleeping, the next capture is a much bigger jump, and
# the encoder falls progressively further behind a scroll it could otherwise track.  Measured on
# a 1280x800 scroll: mean PSNR against the source during the scroll is 16.6 dB at 150 KB/s and
# 55.7 dB at 600 KB/s, for 112 -> 166 KB/s of actual traffic.  It was never a bandwidth wall; it
# was a self-imposed clock.
#
#   PROFILES                 TARGET_KBS  MAX_FRAME_KB  FPS  CLEANUP_MS   scroll / flick PSNR
#   responsive  (LIVE)              600           128   30         400     55.7 / 35.5 dB
#   balanced                        300            64   24         600     50.9 / 12.9 dB
#   sharp       (previous)          150            32   24         700     16.6 / 11.6 dB
#
# All three are identical at rest (~55.5 dB) and all decode bit-exactly against libvpx.  "sharp"
# is the old behaviour: the fewest bytes and the sharpest individual macroblock, at the price of
# showing only part of the screen while anything is moving.
#
# VIDEO_BACKLOG_QI (0 = off) is the lever for a link that genuinely cannot do more than
# ~150 KB/s: when a frame wants more than VIDEO_BACKLOG_X times its budget, code the WHOLE
# screen at that coarse quantizer instead of a fraction of it sharply, and let the idle
# refinement sharpen it afterwards.  Measured at 150 KB/s with a 64 KB frame cap it lifts the
# scroll from 18.4 to 37.5 dB — but peak frame size roughly doubles, so only reach for it when
# raising VIDEO_TARGET_KBS is not an option.

# ---- responsive ----
export VIDEO_QI=8
export VIDEO_MAX_QI=44
export VIDEO_FPS=30
export VIDEO_TARGET_KBS=600
export VIDEO_MAX_FRAME_KB=128
export VIDEO_CLEANUP_MS=400
export VIDEO_BACKLOG_QI=0
export VIDEO_BACKLOG_X=2
